-- EXPECT-ERROR: cannot be widened
-- R4a — THE DECISIVE CASE: a widen pinned by LATER unification (2026-08-06).
--
-- The mirror of reject/t143 (R3's deferred-zonk case), and here for the same
-- structural reason. `cap_narrow`'s result type is a bare `TVar` at the
-- application site; nothing fixes it until something downstream does.
--
-- Below, `narrowed` is unconstrained at the `cap_narrow(c)` call. It is pinned
-- to `Cap(IO.FileWrite)` only by the LATER call to `wants_write`. An EAGER
-- subsumption check reads an unsolved variable at the application site, has no
-- target to compare against, and either reports nothing or (if written to fail
-- closed on unknowns) fires on every legitimate narrowing instead.
--
-- So this file forces the check to be a deferred end-of-module sweep over
-- recorded application sites, zonking both sides then — exactly the mechanism
-- R3 needed, for exactly the same reason. It also pins the fail-CLOSED
-- decision: an unpinned side is not silence here. In R3 an unresolved
-- `from_json` result meant the program never used it as a capability, so
-- silence was right; a narrowing whose direction cannot be determined is the
-- forge itself, so it is refused.
--
-- If this file passes while reject/t153 fails, the sweep is eager and the
-- deferred route is open.
mod DeferredWiden do
  needs IO

  pfn wants_write(w : Cap(IO.FileWrite)) : Int do 1 end

  pfn launder(c : Cap(IO.Console)) : Int do
    let narrowed = cap_narrow(c)
    wants_write(narrowed)
  end

  fn main(cap : Cap(IO)) do
    println("the widen is only visible after later unification")
  end
end
